### B. Initialize through the existing startup lifecycle

Create `Observability/MobileObservability.swift`:

```swift
import DatadogCore
import DatadogRUM
import DatadogCrashReporting

@MainActor
final class MobileObservability {
    static let shared = MobileObservability()
    private var initialized = false
    private init() {}

    func start(
        core: Datadog.Configuration,
        applicationID: String,
        sessionSampleRate: Float,
        consent: TrackingConsent,
        enableNativeCrashes: Bool
    ) {
        guard !initialized else { return }
        precondition(!applicationID.isEmpty)
        precondition(sessionSampleRate.isFinite && (0...100).contains(sessionSampleRate))

        Datadog.initialize(with: core, trackingConsent: consent)
        var rum = RUM.Configuration(applicationID: applicationID)
        rum.sessionSampleRate = sessionSampleRate
        RUM.enable(with: rum)
        if enableNativeCrashes {
            CrashReporting.enable()
        }
        initialized = true
    }
}
```

From your existing AppDelegate or SwiftUI application startup, construct `Datadog.Configuration(clientToken:env:site:service:)` using approved values, then pass it to `start`. Supply actual consent and sampling values, and the approved `enableNativeCrashes` setting. Do not use a default production token or automatically grant consent. Keep this startup on the main actor. [Core and RUM setup](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/ios/setup/).

Xcode scheme environment variables are insufficient for store-launched apps. Bind configuration through the application’s existing release configuration mechanism.

After initialization, apply consent changes with:

```swift
Datadog.set(trackingConsent: newConsent)
```

`newConsent` comes from the app's consent flow. `.pending` allows local collection/batching; `.notGranted` prevents collection. [iOS advanced configuration](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/ios/advanced_configuration/).

