title: "Monitoring & Troubleshooting"
description: "Find mobile events, investigate crashes and slow journeys, and diagnose missing Datadog telemetry."
sidebar:
  label: Monitoring & Troubleshooting
  order: 3
---

import { Tabs, TabItem } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import MermaidZoom from '../components/mermaid-zoom.astro';

**Before starting:** complete [Datadog Mobile Integration](./datadog-mobile-integration.mdx), run a non-production build, and record its environment, app version/build, platform, and test time.

**Outcome:** locate the test journey, identify which layer failed, and give the responsible owner useful evidence.

> **Confidential — Enterprise internal use only.** Use synthetic data. Inspect diagnostic logs locally and redact them before sharing. Never share tokens, signed artifact URLs, customer/order payloads, payment information, or unrestricted session recordings.

## 1. Find your test journey

1. Open Datadog through Enterprise SSO and navigate to **RUM Explorer**.
2. Select the RUM application configured in your build. Set the time range to include your test, accounting for the displayed time zone.
3. Select **View** events. Filter by environment/version using the available facets and autocomplete.
4. Find the **Chicken Order** view from the manual integration example. If you use automatic navigation tracking, use the actual configured route name.
5. Open the view and inspect its related actions/resources/errors. Find **chicken_order.confirmed** after the successful order receipt.
6. Inspect the action attributes. The example sends only `item_count` and `outcome`; it must not contain customer, payment, or order payloads.

The application selector, event type, and time range are separate from the search query. A correct query in the wrong application or time range still returns nothing. [RUM Explorer](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/explorer/), [event inspection](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/explorer/events/).

### Search examples

Replace the environment and version markers with the values actually reported by the app. Select the matching event type first. Confirm attribute names against an event and the search autocomplete.

```text
# View events — ordering screen
 env:REPLACE_ENV version:REPLACE_VERSION @view.name:"Chicken Order"

# Action events — completed order
 env:REPLACE_ENV version:REPLACE_VERSION @action.name:chicken_order.confirmed
```

Copy only the query line, not its comment. Quote values containing spaces or search syntax characters. If an attribute is not available as a searchable facet in your application, use the existing fields or ask the observability owner to configure the appropriate facet. [RUM search syntax](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/explorer/search_syntax/), [event selection and facets](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/explorer/search/).

**Expected result:** the expected ordering view and confirmed action belong to the intended application, environment, and version. Event visibility proves those events arrived; it does not prove every session, crash, or network request is captured.

**Attach screenshot — sanitized ordering view, confirmed action, and environment/version/time filters.**

## 2. Diagnose missing telemetry in order

<MermaidZoom title="Find the failing layer before changing instrumentation">

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["Expected event missing"] --> B{"Correct app, event type and time range?"}
    B -->|No| C["Correct Explorer filters"]
    B -->|Yes| D{"SDK initialized and view tracking called?"}
    D -->|No| E["Fix startup or screen lifecycle binding"]
    D -->|Yes| F{"Consent permits upload and session sampled?"}
    F -->|No| G["Verify approved consent and sampling configuration"]
    F -->|Yes| H["Inspect site, client token, app ID and local SDK diagnostics"]
    H --> I["Check network, proxy and intake response"]
    I --> J["Reproduce with one controlled non-production journey"]
    J --> K["Escalate sanitized evidence if unresolved"]
    classDef start fill:#172554,stroke:#93c5fd,color:#fff;
    classDef decision fill:#713f12,stroke:#fde047,color:#fff;
    classDef fix fill:#4c1d95,stroke:#d8b4fe,color:#fff;
    classDef result fill:#064e3b,stroke:#6ee7b7,color:#fff;
    class A start;
    class B,D,F decision;
    class C,E,G,H,I fix;
    class J,K result;
```

</MermaidZoom>

| Observation | Inspect | Corrective action |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| No events | Application/site/token/ID, initialization, views, consent, sampling | Fix the specific binding; do not create a duplicate RUM application. |
| View exists, confirmed action absent | Receipt validation and success handler | Confirm the action runs after valid success while the intended view is active. |
| Duplicate actions/views | Repeated render effects, duplicate lifecycle handlers, manual plus automatic tracking | Keep one tracking strategy for each screen and one confirmation emission per handled success. |
| Events arrive later | SDK batching, connectivity, device state | Allow normal batching and retest online. Do not repeatedly initialize the SDK. |
| HTTP intake rejection | Sanitized status and SDK error category | Check the matched site/token/application configuration. Never print the token to diagnose it. |
| Debug works, release does not | Native rebuild, release configuration, consent, sampling, network restrictions | Compare actual configuration sources and resolved dependencies. Do not rely on development-only environment variables. |

Use the SDK guide matching the installed version: [Android setup](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/android/setup/), [iOS troubleshooting](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/ios/troubleshooting/), [React Native troubleshooting](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/react_native/troubleshooting/).

## 3. Collect platform diagnostics locally

Enable detailed SDK diagnostics only for the controlled non-production investigation. Restore normal verbosity afterwards. Do not upload unreviewed console output or modify installed SDK source files to add logging.

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="Android">

### Confirm the application process

From a terminal with the project's Android SDK tools on `PATH`:

```bash
adb devices

# Replace with the installed application's actual package ID.
APP_PACKAGE='REPLACE_WITH_APPLICATION_ID'
adb shell pidof "$APP_PACKAGE"
```

If no PID appears, launch the app on the selected device and retry. With multiple devices, select the intended device with `adb -s SERIAL`.

In Android Studio, open **Logcat**, select that device/process, and filter for Datadog SDK diagnostics. Record the error category/status and time; redact configuration and user data. Also confirm the release manifest permits network access and the actual OkHttp client is instrumented. [Android integration](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/android/setup/).

### Confirm the resolved SDK

From the directory containing `gradlew`:

```bash
./gradlew :app:dependencyInsight \
  --configuration debugRuntimeClasspath \
  --dependency dd-sdk-android-rum
```

Replace the module/configuration for the failing variant. This can resolve dependencies; it does not prove event delivery. Check compatible module versions rather than adding a second SDK initialization.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS">

### Inspect SDK diagnostics

Temporarily add this beside the existing SDK initialization in a debug build:

```swift
import DatadogCore

#if DEBUG
Datadog.verbosityLevel = .debug
#endif
```

Run the app from Xcode, exercise the journey, and inspect Datadog messages in the console. Keep the setting out of release diagnostics. [iOS SDK troubleshooting](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/ios/troubleshooting/).

### Compare crash symbol identifiers

On macOS, supply the executable and dSYM from the **same device archive**:

```bash
APP_EXECUTABLE='REPLACE_WITH_ARCHIVED_APP_EXECUTABLE_PATH'
DSYM_BUNDLE='REPLACE_WITH_MATCHING_DSYM_BUNDLE_PATH'
xcrun dwarfdump --uuid "$APP_EXECUTABLE"
xcrun dwarfdump --uuid "$DSYM_BUNDLE"
```

Compare UUIDs for the relevant architecture with the crash's binary identity. A simulator archive or another release's dSYM is not a substitute. Check that the matching file reached Datadog; rerunning upload with an unrelated dSYM cannot repair the stack. [iOS crash reporting and dSYMs](https://docs.datadoghq.com/error_tracking/frontend/mobile/ios/).

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="React Native">

### Inspect the installed integration

From an npm-managed project root:

```bash
npm ls @datadog/mobile-react-native @datadog/mobile-react-navigation --depth=0
```

Use the repository's selected package manager equivalent. The navigation package is optional when using manual view tracking. After adding/updating native modules, rebuild Android/iOS; reloading Metro alone is insufficient. [React Native setup](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/react_native/setup/?tab=rum).

For the provider API in the integration page, temporarily include debug verbosity in its options:

```typescript
import { SdkVerbosity } from '@datadog/mobile-react-native';

// Before mounting the provider, only in the diagnostic build:
configuration.verbosity = SdkVerbosity.DEBUG;
```

`configuration` is the existing result of `createDatadogConfiguration(...)`; do not create a second provider. Inspect the Android process logs or Xcode console for the platform being tested. Remove temporary verbosity after the investigation. Check the installed version's configuration API rather than copying an older constructor. [SDK troubleshooting](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/react_native/troubleshooting/).

### Distinguish JavaScript and native crash symbols

| Stack | Artifact to inspect |
| :--- | :--- |
| Minified JavaScript | Matching platform bundle and source map, with the required release/debug identity. |
| Android obfuscated Java/Kotlin | Exact R8/ProGuard mapping for that variant. |
| iOS native crash | dSYMs matching the crashed binary UUIDs. |

Do not regenerate source maps from a later checkout and assume they match a shipped bundle. For an OTA update, follow the approved integration's update identity rules. [React Native symbolication](https://docs.datadoghq.com/error_tracking/frontend/mobile/reactnative/).

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

## 4. Investigate a slow journey

1. Open the affected application/version and select a time range containing the report.
2. Find the affected view and compare its events with a known working session using the same measurement scope.
3. Inspect available rendering, long-task, CPU, and memory information. Availability depends on platform and SDK support.
4. Inspect resource duration and failures. Follow an eligible resource into APM to investigate backend work.
5. Compare releases and device/OS groups before assigning a cause. A slow network resource does not by itself prove a UI-thread problem.

Use **TTID ≤ 800 ms**, **TTFD ≤ 2 s**, **slow frames ≤ 5%**, **sessions with frozen frames ≤ 0.1%**, and **crash-free sessions ≥ 99.85%** only with the measurement definitions agreed in [Start Here](./index.mdx). Do not substitute browser timings for mobile launch measurements.

Datadog classifies frames taking longer than **700 ms** as frozen. A frame count and the percentage of sessions containing frozen frames require different calculations. [Mobile Vitals](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/application_monitoring/mobile_vitals/).

**Attach screenshot — sanitized affected view and available performance timeline, with version and time filters.**

## 5. Follow a request into backend APM

1. Open a resource associated with the affected RUM view.
2. Confirm it is the intended first-party request using its sanitized URL, method, status, and time.
3. Open its trace link if available. Ask the backend owner to inspect the corresponding service and spans.
4. If no trace link exists, verify the mobile host allowlist, actual network client instrumentation, trace propagation through gateways, backend instrumentation, and sampling/retention.

Do not widen the host allowlist to every destination or inject trace headers into third-party requests to repair missing correlation. Trace headers connect telemetry; they are not authentication credentials. [RUM and APM correlation](https://docs.datadoghq.com/tracing/other_telemetry/rum/).

## 6. Use dashboards and configure alerts

### Open the application dashboard

1. Open the application's RUM performance/summary dashboard.
2. Select the intended application, environment, version, and time range.
3. Inspect stability, available mobile vitals, and resource failures.
4. Use deployment tracking to compare releases with comparable traffic and sampling. Drill into an affected event before concluding that a release caused a regression.

[Mobile performance dashboards](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/platform/dashboards/performance/), [deployment tracking](https://docs.datadoghq.com/real_user_monitoring/platform/dashboards/testing_and_deployment/).

**Attach screenshot — sanitized mobile performance dashboard and release comparison.**

### Configure a monitor with the observability owner

1. Open **Monitors → New Monitor → Real User Monitoring** if your role is authorized to create it; otherwise give the owner the intended measurement.
2. Select event type, application/environment filters, and the actual supported count/measure or formula.
3. Set the owner-approved evaluation window, aggregation, threshold, and grouping. For a percentage, define both numerator and denominator; for timing, define the statistic being evaluated.
4. Inspect the query preview using representative traffic. Define the behavior when traffic is low or data is absent.
5. Add the approved notification destination, responsible team, and investigation link. Validate the notification route through the agreed non-production procedure before operational use.

There is no universal monitor YAML in this section: metric fields, windows, and notification destinations must match the application and approved monitoring configuration. [RUM monitor setup](https://docs.datadoghq.com/monitors/types/real_user_monitoring/), [monitor configuration](https://docs.datadoghq.com/monitors/configuration/).

**No data does not mean healthy.** A zero-count result, an absent group, and a missing measure can behave differently. Confirm the configured missing-data behavior against the monitor's query type. [Monitor missing-data options](https://docs.datadoghq.com/monitors/guide/monitor_api_options/).

**Attach screenshot — approved monitor scope, evaluation settings, and notification destination, with sensitive details hidden.**

## 7. Escalate with useful evidence

Send a sanitized report through the approved internal support route. Do not send raw logs or credentials. Use this template:

```text
Issue: [Missing events / unreadable crash / slow view / missing trace]
Application and platform: [Approved app identity; Android / iOS / React Native]
Environment and version/build: [Exact values]
Observed time and time zone: [Start–end]
SDK versions: [Resolved versions]
Expected event or behavior: [View/action/resource]
Observed result: [What actually appeared]
Reproduction: [Synthetic steps]
Evidence: [Access-controlled event/dashboard/build links]
Diagnostics: [Sanitized status/category and symbol identity if relevant]
Changes already tried: [Specific configuration or code changes]
Owner needed: [Mobile / backend / observability / platform]
```

For missing events, include the Explorer filters and time range. For unreadable crashes, include release/artifact identity. For missing traces, include the sanitized request time and resource evidence. Share session/event identifiers only through approved access-controlled channels.

Return to [Datadog Mobile Integration](./datadog-mobile-integration.mdx) for instrumentation changes or [Start Here](./index.mdx) for access and configuration ownership.









