import { Tabs, TabItem } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import MermaidZoom from '../components/mermaid-zoom.astro';

Use this section to configure **GitHub Copilot or ChatGPT** with the engineering rules for your mobile project. Start here, prepare the shared rules, then follow the setup page for your tool.

**Outcome:** developers can supply consistent instructions covering architecture, platform code, testing, CI/CD, and observability, then verify how the assistant applies them.

> **Confidential — Enterprise internal use only.** Use approved accounts, integrations, and permitted project content. Never provide secrets, production/customer data, signing credentials, or unapproved source code. Instruction files guide responses; they do not enable data protection, enforce permissions, or replace review and CI gates.

## 1. Follow these four pages

| Order | File | What you will do |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| 1 | `index.mdx` | Choose the tool, identify applicable documentation, and understand the setup and verification flow. |
| 2 | `engineering-rules.mdx` | Copy the shared rules and applicable Android, iOS, or React Native additions. Bind them to the repository's real paths and commands. |
| 3 | `github-copilot-setup.mdx` | Configure repository instructions, inspect their use, and run a controlled verification prompt. |
| 4 | `chatgpt-setup.mdx` | Configure project instructions and permitted reference files, start a project chat, and verify the context used. |

Follow page 2, then page 3 or 4. Complete both tool pages only if you use both tools. Detailed test-generation examples remain in [Testing & Quality](../testing-quality/ide-assisted-test-generation.mdx).

## 2. Understand the rules and reference documents

Keep shared engineering requirements separate from task-specific requests. Maintain one reviewed set of engineering requirements. Keep always-on tool instructions concise and provide detailed references for the task. Update each configured copy when its requirements change; do not paste the entire documentation into every prompt.

| Layer | Include | Example |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Shared rules | Requirements applicable to mobile engineering work. | Protect credentials; respect architecture boundaries; verify changes. |
| Platform additions | Requirements for the actual stack. | Kotlin/Android, Swift/iOS, or React Native with Redux Toolkit. |
| Project facts | Verified repository paths, commands, interfaces, and dependency versions. | Actual app module, Xcode scheme, test targets, and lockfile. |
| Task context | The smallest permitted context needed for one change. | Order feature interface, fake gateway, acceptance behavior, existing tests. |

A rule telling the assistant to “read the documentation” is insufficient when the tool cannot access those pages. Provide the permitted relevant content through the tool's supported context mechanism. Never assume a referenced private URL or local path has been read.

### What the shared rules will cover

| Documentation area | Requirement carried into the rules | Reference |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Onboarding | Use the documented macOS/Apple silicon setup; discover real project commands rather than guessing. | [Onboarding](../onboarding/index.mdx). |
| Architecture | Preserve dependency boundaries, feature responsibilities, and established resilience/feature-flag behavior. | [Architecture](../architecture/index.mdx). |
| Platform standards | Follow the selected platform's architecture, state ownership, concurrency, and code conventions. | Platform tabs below. |
| App foundations | Preserve established application initialization, authentication/session handling, and shared foundation contracts. | [App Foundations](../enterprise-app-foundations.mdx). |
| Internal SDKs | Use documented SDK contracts; identify missing API information rather than fabricate it. | [SDK catalog](../master-sdk-catalog.mdx). |
| Branching and PRs | Use the actual repository branch rules, required reviews, and checks; do not bypass them. | [Branching & PRs](../architecture/branching-prs.mdx). |
| CI/CD | Preserve governed enrollment, protected credentials, approved workflows, and evidence-based delivery. Do not treat compilation as verified publication. | [CI/CD](../cicd/index.mdx). |
| Testing & Quality | Add meaningful tests and apply the defined 100% production-code coverage requirement. Keep IDE generation separate from CI execution. | [Testing & Quality](../testing-quality/index.mdx). |
| Observability | Use permitted telemetry fields, approved configuration and consent, and matching release symbols. | [Observability](../observability/index.mdx). |
| Documentation | Write short, actionable steps; include real file locations, commands, replacement values, and expected results. | The applicable feature/setup page. |

Add requirements for other areas only when their approved documentation is available. Do not infer App Store, notification, or deep-linking policies from a sample or an incomplete section.

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="Android">

Use the shared rules plus [Android Standards](../native-android-standards.mdx).

Provide the actual app module, package name, variants, existing Kotlin/test conventions, and local verification commands. Preserve the project's JUnit/Espresso test infrastructure and architecture boundaries.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS">

Use the shared rules plus [iOS Standards](../native-ios-standards.mdx).

Provide the actual workspace/project, scheme, targets, Swift/concurrency requirements, and existing unit/UI test setup. Do not invent provisioning settings, accessibility identifiers, or release configuration bindings.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="React Native">

Use the shared rules plus [React Native Standards](../react-native-standards.mdx).

Preserve **Redux Toolkit** and the existing JavaScript test runner. Keep Android's JUnit-based native device tests distinct from JavaScript tests and JUnit XML reports. React Native is the preferred stack for new applications, subject to the documented stack-selection process; that preference is not permission to migrate an existing app.

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

## 3. Choose how the tool receives instructions

| Tool | Instruction location | Reference context |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
