Use an approved **ChatGPT project** to keep engineering instructions and permitted references together across related chats.

**You need:** access to the approved ChatGPT account/workspace, permission to use the selected project content, and the reviewed `docs/engineering-rules.md` assembled in [Engineering Rules](./engineering-rules.mdx).

**Outcome:** a project containing concise instructions, the applicable engineering rules, and enough verified context for a bounded development task.

> **Confidential — Enterprise internal use only.** Use approved accounts and permitted content. Never upload credentials, signing files, customer/production data, private logs, or an unapproved repository archive. These instructions do not enable data protection, alter retention, authorize sharing, or replace workspace administration.

**Scope:** this page configures a ChatGPT project with instructions and supplied references. It does not configure an IDE extension, repository agent, or local-computer access. No API key, npm package, or application dependency is needed for this setup.

## 1. Open the approved ChatGPT environment

1. Sign in through the Enterprise-approved account and access process. Confirm you are in the intended workspace before adding project content.
2. Open **Projects** and create a project for the application, or select its existing approved project.
3. Confirm the project's intended audience/access before adding references. Do not make a confidential project broadly accessible merely to simplify setup.
4. Open the project's instruction editor. Menu labels vary by client; use the **project-level** instructions, not an ordinary message in a standalone chat.

A project groups related chats, files, instructions, and sources. Project instructions apply across its chats. [Official ChatGPT project guidance](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/projects?surface=app).

If projects/instructions are unavailable in the approved environment, ask the workspace owner to confirm the supported configuration. Do not switch to a personal account or an unapproved integration.

**Attach screenshot — selected project and instruction editor, with account, workspace, and private project details hidden.**

## 2. Prepare and add the reference content

Use the following separation. The project and file names are suggested documentation conventions, not automatic configuration discovery rules.

```text
ChatGPT project: <approved application project>
├── Project instructions         # Copy Section 3 into the instruction editor
├── Project sources/references
│   ├── engineering-rules.md     # Reviewed shared rules + relevant platform rules
│   └── ...permitted standards and contracts for this project...
└── Chats
    ├── Rules verification       # Controlled planning prompt
    └── <one bounded task>       # Relevant task-specific context
```

1. Review `docs/engineering-rules.md` locally. Complete verified project facts and keep unresolved contracts explicit.
2. Add the permitted copy to the project's **Sources/files** area. Use an approved connected source only when its access and content are authorized.
3. Add the applicable platform standard and architecture reference when needed. Include App Foundations and SDK contracts when the change uses them. Add CI/CD, testing, or observability references for tasks touching those areas.
4. For one-off tasks, attach only the relevant permitted implementation, interface, fixture, and test files to that task's chat.
5. Identify the revision of the provided references. Remove or clearly supersede stale uploaded project copies so the intended version is unambiguous; do not delete or modify original connected documents as part of this setup.

Project sources can be available across project chats; a chat attachment may be specific to that interaction. A ChatGPT project does not automatically read a local repository folder. Providing a private URL also does not prove ChatGPT can retrieve its contents. [Project sources and chat context](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/projects?surface=app), [working with supplied context](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/use-chatgpt).

Saving `.github/copilot-instructions.md` or `docs/engineering-rules.md` locally does not automatically configure this ChatGPT project. Save the project instructions and explicitly provide the permitted reference content.

Do not upload all documentation or source files merely to make the project “complete.” Supply the permitted material relevant to the application and task. Do not add source connections solely because they are technically available.

### Identify each reference revision

Provide this record with the permitted files or in task context. Replace markers with verified values; report unavailable revisions as `unknown`.

```text
Engineering rules: engineering-rules.md — REPLACE_WITH_REVIEWED_REVISION
Platform standard: REPLACE_WITH_PROVIDED_FILE_AND_REVISION
Feature/test context: REPLACE_WITH_FILES_AND_REPOSITORY_COMMIT
Unresolved contracts: REPLACE_WITH_KNOWN_GAPS_OR_NONE
```

## 3. Copy the project instructions

Paste the following **Markdown contents**, without the enclosing code fences, into the project's instruction editor and save them. This is an instruction template, not an API request or executable configuration.

```markdown
# Enterprise mobile engineering instructions

## Context and authority
- Use the provided engineering-rules.md, applicable platform additions, and verified
  project facts for this application. Inspect the relevant content before relying
  on it; do not claim access to an unavailable file, private link, or local folder.
- Distinguish approved project requirements from illustrative samples. Never invent
  an internal SDK method, endpoint, YAML field, selector, command, or business rule.
- Identify missing or conflicting contracts. Ask for the specific permitted context
  or owning-team decision needed, while keeping independent work within scope.
- Use the reference revision supplied for the task. Do not silently combine
  contradictory requirements from old and current documents.

## Engineering requirements
- Preserve Clean Architecture, unidirectional data flow, lifecycle/cancellation,
  account scope, and approved authentication/application foundation contracts.
- Apply the platform rules for the files being changed. Preserve Redux Toolkit for
  React Native; distinguish JavaScript tests from Android JUnit native device tests
  and from JUnit XML reports. Do not replace the established test framework.
- Use approved compatible dependencies and existing project commands. Keep changes
  focused; do not add unrelated packages, migrations, or framework replacements.
- Add meaningful assertions and the required failure cases. Apply the documented
  100% production-code coverage scope and supported metrics without hiding logic.
- AI drafts code at authoring time only. CI executes committed, reviewed code;
  do not add an LLM generation or automatic AI repair step to Bitrise.
- Preserve governed enrollment, protected credentials, release routes, and evidence
  requirements. For uncertain external writes, follow read-only reconciliation;
  do not prescribe blind retries, duplicate provisioning, or resource deletion.
- Keep telemetry fields permitted. Sampling is not redaction. Never place a Datadog
  API key in an application or expose credentials in diagnostic output.

## Confidentiality and scope
- Use only the permitted context. Never request or expose secrets, signing material,
  production/customer data, private logs, or unapproved source code.
- Do not infer authorization to connect services, create cloud resources, upload
  artifacts, change access, send messages, or deploy from a request for code examples.
- Use synthetic fixtures and approved fake services. Never submit real orders or
  payments to validate a generated example.

## Output and verification
- Provide actual file locations, coherent code, required imports, replacement values,
  integration points, and the applicable verification commands when known.
- Identify partial snippets and host-provided interfaces explicitly. Do not label an
  example as directly runnable when required bindings or contracts are missing.
- State what was inspected, changed, and actually executed. If execution is unavailable,
  label code as unverified; do not claim builds, tests, coverage, or deployment passed.
- Keep explanations short and actionable. Do not add checklists, filler, invented
  policies, or internal preparation/review commentary to published documentation.
- For documentation diagrams, use the existing MermaidZoom component only. Do not
  claim rendering, expansion, or light/dark verification without checking them.
```

After saving, reopen the project instructions to confirm the intended text is present.

This compact entry point complements the full shared rules; it does not replace their platform-specific requirements. The filename `engineering-rules.md` must match the reference you actually provide. A file mention alone does not ensure its relevant contents were used in a response.

**Expected result:** instructions are saved at project level and the intended reviewed rule document is present among the project's permitted references.

## 4. Select the platform context

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="Android">

Provide permitted excerpts from [Android Standards](../native-android-standards.mdx), the actual module/variant configuration, and the relevant Kotlin/domain/test files.

```text
Platform: Native Android
Use the supplied Kotlin/Compose/ViewModel/StateFlow conventions and
existing repository contracts. Preserve coroutine cancellation and account scope.
For tests, use the actual unit target and JUnit/Espresso infrastructure.
Identify any missing resource IDs, idling setup, variant, or Gradle task.
```

Keep the actual Gradle/JDK/AGP/Kotlin compatibility set. Do not let an unrelated online snippet choose new toolchain versions.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS">

Provide permitted excerpts from [iOS Standards](../native-ios-standards.mdx), the actual workspace/scheme/targets, and relevant Swift/domain/test files.

```text
Platform: Native iOS
Use the supplied Swift and SwiftUI/UIKit conventions and existing contracts.
Preserve actor isolation, Sendable boundaries, task ownership, and cancellation.
Use the actual unit and XCUITest targets and accessibility identifiers.
Identify missing deployment, scheme, fixture, or test-command information.
```

The reference example's deployment target or concurrency settings must not silently replace the application's approved configuration.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="React Native">

Provide permitted excerpts from [React Native Standards](../react-native-standards.mdx), the actual package/lockfile configuration, Redux Toolkit feature, and relevant tests.

```text
Platform: React Native
Use strict TypeScript, Redux Toolkit, and the supplied feature contracts.
Preserve the existing JavaScript test runner and Android JUnit native device suite.
Do not add Appium/WebdriverIO or replace the project's state library.
Identify missing native bindings, accessibility mappings, fixtures, and commands.
```

Include Android/iOS context when changing native integrations. A JavaScript-only file set cannot establish the native build or device-test configuration.

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

## 5. Verify the project before requesting implementation

<MermaidZoom title="Verify instructions and references before using a generated change">

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["Save project instructions"] --> B["Add permitted engineering rules and feature context"]
    B --> C["Start a fresh chat inside this project"]
    C --> D["Request a plan with no actions"]
    D --> E["Inspect referenced content, platform and missing facts"]
    E --> F{"Does the plan follow the supplied requirements?"}
