---

import { Tabs, TabItem } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import MermaidZoom from '../components/mermaid-zoom.astro';

Start here when you have a mobile application and need to distribute it to employees, business testers, or external users. Identify the audience first, then follow the approval and store-specific procedure. Automated build/upload configuration stays in [CI/CD](../cicd/index.mdx).

**Outcome:** select the correct distribution route and prepare a release handoff that the release owner can validate.

> **Confidential — Enterprise internal use only.** Keep internal store URLs, QR codes, reviewer credentials, signing material, artifacts, and administrative evidence in approved systems. Use redacted screenshots; never publish credentials or private artifact links in documentation.

## 1. Choose the destination before preparing a release

| Intended audience | Enterprise route | Required decision |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Development and QA team | Internal QA store/catalog. | Correct environment, application, and test audience. |
| Business users validating the app | Internal UAT store/catalog. | Business audience and required TGRC approval before UAT deployment. |
| Employees using the production app | Internal production store/catalog. | Production audience, access rules, and required TGRC approval. |
| Customers or other external users | Public Google Play / Apple App Store route. | Store account, application identity, country availability, review, and release authorization. |

These are the documented Enterprise routes. The release/platform owner confirms the route for the application; a developer must not choose an incompatible signing or distribution mechanism as a shortcut.

For iOS, Apple Developer Enterprise Program distribution is for eligible proprietary internal employee use. It is not the public App Store distribution path. [Apple Enterprise Program](https://developer.apple.com/programs/enterprise/).

**Google Play internal testing and TestFlight are public-store platform testing channels. They are separate from the Enterprise internal QA/UAT/production catalog.** Do not infer one channel's publication or access from another channel's successful upload.

## 2. Follow this section in order

| Order | File | What it covers |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| 1 | `index.mdx` | Choose the audience/destination and understand the release journey. |
| 2 | `signing-and-release-approval.mdx` | Application ID conventions, signing ownership, certificate requests, TGRC approval, deployment request form, spreadsheet attachment, and secure handoff. |
| 3 | `enterprise-app-store.mdx` | QA/UAT/production stores, user access and entitlements, approved portal/QR installation, iOS trust guidance, and version visibility. |
| 4 | `apple-app-store.mdx` | Listing fields, devices/languages, SKU, privacy, countries, screenshots/previews/icons, reviewer access/notes, signed IPA transfer, Transporter, upload processing, review, and release. |
| 5 | `google-play.mdx` | APK/AAB and signing handoff, listing fields/assets/category/privacy, reviewer access, initial upload readiness, testing tracks, review, and release. |
| 6 | `lost-device-data-wipe.mdx` | Administrator-only Digital.ai/Apperian procedure, exact user/device/application selection, policy evaluation, local-data scope, user notification, and outcome verification. |

Read **Signing & Release Approval** next, then the page for your destination. Use **Lost-Device Data Wipe** only for the authorized administrator procedure.

Store pages explain requirements, handoff, console actions, and verification. The approved deployment step YAML remains in [Delivery & Verification](../cicd/delivery-and-verification.mdx); protected workflow credential binding remains in [Signing & Credentials](../cicd/signing-and-credentials.mdx).

## 3. Confirm ownership and approval

| Responsibility | Required handoff |
| :--- | :--- |
| Developer | Correct app identity, tested source/build, supported devices, version/build, and requested release changes. |
| Business/application owner | Intended audience, application content, country availability, and business access decisions. |
| Release/platform owner | Approved signing, destination account, secure artifact handling, and release execution. |
| TGRC/security approval owner | Required approval for deployment to UAT or production through the corporate process. |
| Store/review owner | Complete store submission, review access, and destination-specific evidence. |

Use the actual assigned owners. The table identifies responsibilities, not new permissions or a replacement approval workflow.

Obtain formal **Technology Governance, Risk & Compliance (TGRC)** approval before deploying to UAT or production. Submit the deployment request through the approved Enterprise IT portal, complete the required request-template spreadsheet, and attach it to the request. The next page explains this handoff. A passing build or automated scan does not replace the approval.

## 4. Identify your platform release inputs

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="Android">

Record the package/application ID, version name, version code, source commit, and requested destination. Use the approved application ID naming convention, `com.enterprise.<app-name>`; confirm the exact identifier and environment mapping with the release owner. Preserve an existing registered application identity.

For public Google Play delivery, confirm the app's **Play App Signing** configuration and the upload-key owner. The upload key and app signing key serve different purposes; do not assume an internal distribution key is the correct Play upload key. The release owner supplies the approved signing process. [Play App Signing](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/9842756).

If the release process accepts an unsigned build as an input, that is a secure handoff **to the authorized signer**, not a file ready to upload to Play. Follow [Signing & Credentials](../cicd/signing-and-credentials.mdx).

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS">

Record the bundle ID, app version, build number, source commit, supported devices, and destination. Confirm the exact approved `com.enterprise.<app-name>` identifier; do not rename an existing registered app merely to match a sample.

Keep internal in-house signing separate from App Store signing. The release/platform owner binds the correct account, distribution identity, provisioning, and export configuration. Request required signing resources through the approved internal service process; do not share raw private keys or certificates in PRs or chats.

App Store Connect accepts supported upload methods including Xcode and Transporter. Upload processing must finish before the build is available for the subsequent submission/testing steps. [Apple build uploads](https://developer.apple.com/help/app-store-connect/manage-builds/upload-builds).

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="React Native">

Prepare **two native release identities** when delivering both platforms:

| Android | iOS |
| :--- | :--- |
| Application ID, version name/code, approved signed artifact and destination. | Bundle ID, app version/build, approved archive/export and destination. |

A JavaScript bundle or Metro build is not an Android/iOS store binary. Apply each platform's signing and store requirements independently. Preserve the exact JS bundle/source maps and native symbols associated with the shipped version for [crash investigation](../observability/monitoring-and-troubleshooting.mdx).

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

## 5. Prepare the release request

Use this short preparation record to gather the information needed by the release owner. It supplements the required request-template spreadsheet; it does not replace that form or define a deployment API. Use approved access-controlled references rather than passwords or private download URLs.

```text
Application: [Approved name]
Audience and destination: [Internal QA/UAT/PROD or named public-store track/release]
Platform: [Android / iOS; separate records when identities differ]
Application ID / Bundle ID: [Actual identifier]
Version and build/code: [Exact values]
Source commit: [Exact commit]
Artifact reference: [Approved access-controlled record]
Artifact SHA-256: [Hash of the actual handoff artifact]
Signing owner/status: [Authorized owner and verified status]
Required approval: [TGRC/release record and state]
Listing/content owner: [Responsible owner]
Reviewer access: [Approved secure reference, if required; never the password]
Requested countries/audience: [Approved scope]
Test/release evidence: [Access-controlled records]
Unresolved items: [Specific gaps and owners]
```

On macOS, compute a hash without uploading the artifact:

```bash
ARTIFACT_PATH='REPLACE_WITH_ACTUAL_HANDOFF_ARTIFACT_PATH'
test -s "$ARTIFACT_PATH" && shasum -a 256 "$ARTIFACT_PATH"
```

Record the hash for the artifact at that stage. Signing, re-signing, or repackaging can change it; link subsequent artifacts to their own identities. A hash does not prove correct signing, approval, or store acceptance.

**Attach screenshot — sanitized release handoff and approval state, with credentials/private links hidden.**

## 6. Follow the release journey

<MermaidZoom title="Build success and user-visible delivery are different milestones">

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["Identify audience and store destination"] --> C["Prepare app identity, release information and signing handoff"]
    C --> B["Complete required request and approvals before UAT / production deployment"]
    B --> D["Upload through the authorized route"]
    D --> E["Confirm processing and destination status"]
    E --> F{"Internal or public store?"}
    F -->|Internal| G["Verify signing, enabled version and assigned audience"]
    F -->|Public| H["Complete required testing, review and release controls"]
    G --> I["Verify exact version is available to the intended user"]
    H --> I
    classDef prepare fill:#172554,stroke:#93c5fd,color:#fff;
    classDef delivery fill:#4c1d95,stroke:#d8b4fe,color:#fff;
    classDef decision fill:#713f12,stroke:#fde047,color:#fff;
    classDef verified fill:#064e3b,stroke:#6ee7b7,color:#fff;
    class A,B,C prepare;
    class D,E,G,H delivery;
    class F decision;
    class I verified;
```

</MermaidZoom>

1. Confirm the audience, environment/store, exact application identifier, and release owner.
2. Prepare the platform artifact handoff and the metadata/assets required by the selected store page.
3. Complete the certificate/deployment requests and required approvals before deployment to UAT or production.
4. The authorized release process uploads the identified candidate and completes destination processing.
5. Verify the resulting version and intended user access at that destination.

Confirm the exact application, version/build, environment/track, and intended audience at the destination. Internal distribution requires the correct enabled version and access assignment; public stores have their own processing, review, testing, and release states.

If a result is missing or uncertain, record it as **unverified**. Do not infer public availability from TestFlight, Google Play internal testing, or a green Bitrise build. Preserve partial results and follow [Safe Recovery](../cicd/troubleshooting-and-recovery.mdx) rather than blindly repeating an external write.

Review duration varies; plan using the actual submission status and current store guidance. Do not promise a fixed Apple or Google review completion date.

**Expected result:** you know the destination, responsible owner, required approval route, and platform/store information to prepare. If any of those is unknown, resolve it with the responsible owner before requesting deployment.

**Next file:** `signing-and-release-approval.mdx` — certificate requests, signing ownership, TGRC approval, and the release handoff.



