---
title: "Signing & Release Approval"
description: "Request the correct signing setup, complete the TGRC deployment request and spreadsheet, and hand the identified candidate to the authorized release owner."
sidebar:
  label: Signing & Release Approval
  order: 2
---

import { Tabs, TabItem } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import MermaidZoom from '../components/mermaid-zoom.astro';

**Starting point:** you know the application, target audience, environment, and destination from [Start Here](./index.mdx).

**Outcome:** the release owner has the correct signing request, required deployment approval, completed request spreadsheet, and identified candidate for the selected destination.

> **Confidential — Enterprise internal use only.** Keep internal request links, artifacts, credentials, and approval evidence in approved systems. Never attach private keys, keystore passwords, signing credentials, or reviewer passwords to source, PRs, screenshots, or AI prompts.

## 1. Identify the destination and application

Before raising a request, record:

| Information | What to confirm |
| :--- | :--- |
| Audience | Employees, QA team, business UAT users, or external users. |
| Environment/store | Internal QA/UAT/production, Google Play, or Apple App Store Connect. |
| Application identity | Exact Android application ID or iOS bundle ID, including the approved environment mapping. |
| Candidate | Source commit, app version, and platform build number/version code. |
| Owner | The authorized release/platform owner handling signing and deployment. |

The documented naming convention is `com.enterprise.<app-name>`. Confirm the exact registered identifier and permitted suffixes with the owner; do not rename an existing application or invent an environment mapping to fit an example.

**Expected result:** the request identifies one application and a specific destination. If either is unknown, resolve it with the application/release owner before requesting a certificate.

## 2. Confirm which signing setup is required

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="Android">

Enterprise Android signing is centrally managed by the release/platform owner. Use the approved existing signing identity; developers must not generate a replacement release key as a setup workaround.

| Destination | Owner must confirm |
| :--- | :--- |
| Internal store | Application ID, APK signing identity, and any App Management signing requirements. |
| Google Play | Application ID, AAB/upload requirements, registered upload certificate, and Play App Signing configuration. |

A centrally maintained distribution identity does not make the Play **upload key** and **app signing key** interchangeable. With Play App Signing, the upload key signs the submitted bundle and Google Play signs delivered APKs with the app signing key. Confirm the actual configuration for this app before using a key. [Play App Signing](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/9842756).

If the release manager requests an unsigned APK/build, transfer it through the approved secure handoff. That input still needs the destination's approved signing process; it is not a ready-to-upload Play candidate.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS">

Choose the signing path by destination:

| Destination | Owner must confirm |
| :--- | :--- |
| Internal in-house distribution | Enterprise account/team, compatible distribution certificate/profile, entitlements, and export method. |
| Public App Store / TestFlight | Appropriate Apple Developer team, store-compatible distribution signing/profile configuration, entitlements, and export method. |

A certificate alone is insufficient: the signing identity includes its private key, and the app/extension identifiers, team, entitlements, profiles, and distribution method must agree. The authorized owner manages these resources. [Apple certificate overview](https://developer.apple.com/help/account/certificates/certificates-overview/).

An App Store Connect API key authorizes supported account/upload operations; it does not replace application code signing. Use the platform-owned credential integration described in [CI/CD App Signing](../cicd/signing-and-credentials.mdx).

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="React Native">

Complete both native paths when releasing both platforms. React Native does not have one shared release certificate.

```text
One application source revision
├── Android: application ID + version code + approved Android signing
└── iOS: bundle ID + build number + approved Apple signing/export
```

Record results separately. A signed Android package does not establish that iOS signing is ready. Keep credentials out of JavaScript, Redux state, Metro configuration, and committed environment files.

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

## 3. Request the certificate/signing setup

**Developer or assigned request author:**

1. Open the approved **Enterprise IT service portal** using the internal link supplied by the release/platform owner.
2. Open the certificate request route designated for the application and deployment target.
3. Provide the platform, exact application identifier, environment/destination, and responsible owner. Complete the actual required portal fields.
4. Record the request reference and follow its status through the portal.
5. Ask the authorized signing owner to confirm the resulting identity and protected build-workflow binding before selecting a release workflow.

Do not invent a portal URL, form field, or request type from the examples. If the link/form is unavailable, obtain it from the responsible owner. The protected certificate-request link belongs in the approved internal portal/document configuration, not in a public example.

**Expected result:** an owner-confirmed signing setup for the intended destination. A submitted request or a downloaded public certificate alone does not establish readiness.

**Attach screenshot — certificate request route and sanitized request/status, with internal links and credential details hidden.**

## 4. Obtain TGRC approval and submit the deployment request

Every application requires formal **Technology Governance, Risk & Compliance (TGRC)** approval before deployment to **UAT or production**. Keep that approval distinct from code review, automated security results, and store review.

1. Obtain the current deployment request-template spreadsheet through the approved Enterprise IT request process or responsible owner.
2. Complete **all required fields in that actual template**. Use the same app identity, destination, and release details as the signing handoff.
3. Open the deployment request in the Enterprise IT portal and complete its required form fields.
4. Attach the completed spreadsheet directly to the request. Use approved references for binaries and test/release evidence; do not put secrets into the spreadsheet.
5. Record the deployment request reference and the TGRC approval reference/state through the established process.
6. Confirm formal approval covers the intended UAT/production deployment before the authorized release process proceeds. Resolve questions or requested corrections with the assigned owner.

This guide does not define spreadsheet columns, portal routing, or TGRC evidence requirements beyond the actual supplied form. Use the current form; do not replace it with a homemade YAML file or the preparation record below.

| State | What it means |
| :--- | :--- |
| Request submitted | A request exists; approval has not been established. |
| Additional information requested | Complete the requested correction through the existing process. |
| Required approval recorded | Confirm its scope matches the intended deployment before release. |
| Approval missing, rejected, or unclear | Do not proceed to UAT/production deployment; resolve with the responsible owner. |

**Attach screenshot — completed template structure and approval status, with sensitive fields and private links hidden.**

## 5. Prepare the secure release handoff

Use this copyable record to communicate with the authorized release owner. It **supplements the required spreadsheet**, not replaces it. Complete one platform record per native candidate where values differ.

```text
Application: [Approved app name]
Platform: [Android / iOS]
Application ID / Bundle ID: [Exact registered identifier]
Audience and environment: [QA / UAT / production and intended users]
Destination: [Internal store / Play track / App Store Connect route]
Source commit: [Exact revision]
Version and build/code: [Actual package values]
Signing request: [Approved internal request reference]
Signing readiness: [Owner-confirmed identity/status or unresolved]
Deployment request: [Approved internal request reference]
Request spreadsheet: [Completed template attachment reference]
TGRC approval: [Approval reference, scope, and actual state]
Artifact: [Approved secure transfer reference; no raw private download URL]
Artifact SHA-256: [Hash of this exact handoff file]
Test/security evidence: [Approved access-controlled references]
Release owner: [Responsible owner]
Unresolved items: [Specific gaps and owners]
```

Transfer binaries using the approved Enterprise file-transfer service, such as the approved Syncplicity route where applicable. Obtain the authorized destination and recipient from the release owner. Do not copy a private artifact URL or signing password into a PR to make the handoff easier.

On macOS, identify the actual handoff file with:

```bash
# Replace the path before running. This only reads the local artifact.
ARTIFACT_PATH='REPLACE_WITH_ACTUAL_HANDOFF_FILE_PATH'
if [ -s "$ARTIFACT_PATH" ]; then
  shasum -a 256 "$ARTIFACT_PATH"
else
  echo 'Handoff artifact is missing or empty.' >&2
fi
```

A hash identifies file contents, not the signer, application identity, or approval state. Signing/re-signing changes the candidate; preserve the relationship between input and output and record the final artifact's own identity.

## 6. Confirm readiness before delivery

<MermaidZoom title="Requests, signing readiness and approval must match the deployment">

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["Confirm application identity and destination"] --> B["Request owner-managed signing setup"]
    A --> C["Complete deployment spreadsheet and portal request"]
    B --> D["Owner confirms compatible signing and protected binding"]
    C --> E["Record required TGRC approval before UAT / production"]
    D --> F["Identify exact candidate and secure handoff"]
    E --> F
    F --> G{"Identity, signing and approvals match?"}
    G -->|No or unknown| H["Resolve with the responsible owner"]
    G -->|Yes| I["Authorized delivery and destination verification"]
    classDef request fill:#172554,stroke:#93c5fd,color:#fff;
    classDef readiness fill:#4c1d95,stroke:#d8b4fe,color:#fff;
    classDef decision fill:#713f12,stroke:#fde047,color:#fff;
    classDef done fill:#064e3b,stroke:#6ee7b7,color:#fff;
    class A,B,C request;
    class D,E,F readiness;
    class G,H decision;
    class I done;
```

</MermaidZoom>

The release owner confirms that signing, approval scope, artifact, application identity, and destination agree. Developers use the approved workflow rather than uploading production signing credentials manually.

| Need | Follow |
| :--- | :--- |
| Protected signing workflow YAML and output verification | [CI/CD App Signing](../cicd/signing-and-credentials.mdx). |
| Automated upload steps and exact-version verification | [Delivery & Verification](../cicd/delivery-and-verification.mdx). |
| Uncertain external write or partial release | [Troubleshooting & Safe Recovery](../cicd/troubleshooting-and-recovery.mdx). |

Store review is a separate process after the applicable submission. Do not treat an internal approval as Apple/Google acceptance or promise a fixed review completion date. Apple uploads also require processing before they become available for subsequent steps. [Apple build processing](https://developer.apple.com/help/app-store-connect/manage-builds/upload-builds).

**Expected result:** the authorized release owner has a complete, consistent handoff and the required approval before deployment. Missing approval, incompatible signing, or an uncertain artifact identity must be resolved before proceeding.

**Next file:** `enterprise-app-store.mdx` — internal environments, entitlement access, installation, and application visibility.




