---
title: "Apple App Store"
description: "Prepare Apple listing information and assets, hand over the signed IPA, upload with Transporter, and verify review and release separately."
sidebar:
  label: Apple App Store
  order: 4
---

import { Tabs, TabItem } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import MermaidZoom from '../components/mermaid-zoom.astro';

Use this page for the **public Apple App Store** route. TestFlight testing and public release are separate outcomes. Internal in-house applications follow [Enterprise App Store](./enterprise-app-store.mdx).

**Before starting:** confirm the app identity, authorized release owner, and required approvals in [Signing & Release Approval](./signing-and-release-approval.mdx).

**Outcome:** complete listing/review information is associated with the correct processed build, then the approved version is released and verified in its intended storefronts.

> **Confidential — Enterprise internal use only.** Transfer the signed IPA through approved secure channels. Keep signing credentials and reviewer passwords out of source, documentation, PRs, screenshots, and ordinary handoff text. The authorized submission owner enters required reviewer credentials into the appropriate restricted review fields.

## 1. Identify the application and store record

1. Confirm the exact bundle ID, app version, build number, supported devices, and source revision with the release owner.
2. Open the intended Enterprise team/provider in **App Store Connect** and locate the existing application.
3. For a new app, the authorized owner creates the app record with the approved platform, name, primary language, bundle ID, and unique SKU. Do not create a duplicate record for an update.
4. Match the record's bundle ID to the candidate. The documented identifier convention is `com.enterprise.<app-name>`; preserve the actual registered identity.

A **SKU** is the app's internal tracking identifier, not its bundle ID or Apple-generated numeric app ID. Confirm a unique approved SKU; do not reuse one shared default across different app records. It cannot be edited after the app record is saved. [Create an app record](https://developer.apple.com/help/app-store-connect/create-an-app-record/add-a-new-app/), [Apple SKU definition](https://developer.apple.com/help/glossary/sku/).

**Attach screenshot — intended provider/app record and sanitized application identity.**

## 2. Prepare product-page information

Complete the actual App Store Connect fields for each supported localization. The business/content owner approves public text; developers verify that it describes the shipped functionality.

| Information | What to prepare |
| :--- | :--- |
| App name / store display name | Exact approved public name; Apple's limit is 2–30 characters. |
| Subtitle | Short summary, up to 30 characters. |
| Keywords | Relevant search terms; Apple's version-information reference allows up to 100 bytes. Do not treat bytes as characters for every language. |
| Description | Plain-text feature/functionality description, up to 4,000 characters. |
| Primary language/localizations | Approved primary language and translated listing content. |
| Supported devices | Actual iPhone/iPad support declared by the application, matching its listing assets. |
| Privacy policy URL | Live, publicly accessible approved policy applicable to the app. |
| Support URL | Working support page with actual contact information, as required by Apple. |
| Countries/regions | Explicit approved availability; do not select every storefront by default. |
| Copyright | Approved year and legal entity name. |

[App information and field limits](https://developer.apple.com/help/app-store-connect/reference/app-information/app-information/), [version metadata and keywords](https://developer.apple.com/help/app-store-connect/reference/app-information/platform-version-information).

Use this **preparation record**, not an executable upload schema:

```text
App Store name: [Approved exact display name]
Subtitle: [Approved localized text]
Keywords: [Approved localized search terms]
Description: [Approved localized feature description]
Primary language: [Actual locale]
Additional listing languages: [Approved locales]
Supported devices: [iPhone / iPad / both, matching the binary]
SKU: [Approved unique value; existing value for updates]
Privacy policy URL: [Approved live public policy]
Support URL: [Approved live public support page]
Countries/regions: [Approved availability]
Copyright: [Year and legal entity]
Content owner: [Responsible owner]
```

**Expected result:** the release owner has all listing content and knows who approved it. Unfilled fields remain unresolved; sample text must not be submitted as production metadata.

## 3. Prepare screenshots, previews, and icons

### Screenshots and app previews

1. Review the current [Apple screenshot specifications](https://developer.apple.com/help/app-store-connect/reference/app-information/screenshot-specifications) for the app's supported device families and required display sizes.
2. Capture the actual candidate's representative screens with synthetic data. Include the required device/localization variants.
3. Prepare **at least four screenshots per target device family** for the Enterprise release handoff. This is the documented internal preparation requirement; Apple's general minimum is one and its maximum is ten screenshots per device size.
4. Use accepted JPEG/JPG/PNG dimensions and formats without transparency, and provide the intended display order. Check required display sizes in the current specification rather than scaling an arbitrary image to fit.
5. If providing optional previews, prepare up to **three per supported device size and language**, following Apple's video specifications.
6. The authorized owner uploads assets into the matching device-size/localization sections of the selected version.

[Upload and order screenshots/previews](https://developer.apple.com/help/app-store-connect/manage-app-information/upload-app-previews-and-screenshots/), [preview specifications](https://developer.apple.com/help/app-store-connect/reference/app-information/app-preview-specifications).

On macOS, inspect an image before handoff:

```bash
SCREENSHOT_PATH='REPLACE_WITH_ACTUAL_SCREENSHOT_PATH'
sips -g pixelWidth -g pixelHeight -g format -g hasAlpha "$SCREENSHOT_PATH"
```

Compare the output to the required Apple specification for that image's device-size slot. This command inspects properties; it does not certify visual quality, privacy, or App Store acceptance.

### App icons and interface quality

Use the approved icon assets and the current platform/toolchain asset workflow. Do not bake in an extra rounded mask or submit a screenshot as an app icon. Check appearances, safe content placement, and target requirements against [Apple app icon guidance](https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/app-icons).

Review the application's navigation, layout, and accessibility against [Apple Human Interface Guidelines](https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/). Listing images must accurately represent the submitted app.

Suggested secure handoff organization—these are documentation folders, not a required upload format:

```text
<approved-release-handoff>/
├── listing/                  # Approved localized text and release notes
├── screenshots/<locale>/    # Device-size groups and display order
├── previews/<locale>/       # Optional videos
├── icon-reference/          # Approved asset/design reference
└── release-record.txt       # Identity, approvals, secure artifact reference
```

Keep the IPA in the approved artifact-transfer location. Do not add credentials or private keys to this handoff directory.

**Attach screenshot — sanitized localized product-page preview, device-size assets, and display order.**

## 4. Complete privacy and reviewer information

### Privacy information

1. Confirm the app's actual data collection/use with the application and privacy owners, including integrated third-party SDKs.
2. Enter the approved **App Privacy** answers in App Store Connect and confirm the policy URL is accessible.
3. Verify relevant permission-purpose strings, privacy manifests/SDK requirements, and tracking declarations against the app's behavior and current Apple requirements. Do not declare “no data collected” solely because custom app logs are empty.

Privacy labels, privacy manifests, permission prompts, and tracking permission are different requirements; one does not automatically satisfy all the others. [Apple App Privacy Details](https://developer.apple.com/app-store/app-privacy-details/), [manage privacy answers](https://developer.apple.com/help/app-store-connect/manage-app-information/manage-app-privacy/).

### Review access and notes

1. Provide a dedicated, active, unlocked test account when sign-in is required for review. Use approved test data and access sufficient to inspect the submitted functionality.
2. Verify the account works against the submitted build and review environment. Explain any special access steps or configuration in review notes.
3. Give the authorized submission owner the credentials through the approved secure channel. Enter them in the appropriate App Review sign-in fields, not in a public listing or screenshot.
4. Provide a short feature walkthrough and any reviewer instructions needed to reach the relevant screens.

[Apple App Review Guidelines](https://developer.apple.com/app-store/review/guidelines/), [review information fields](https://developer.apple.com/help/app-store-connect/reference/app-information/platform-version-information).

**Attach screenshot — sanitized privacy/review sections, with all credentials hidden.**

## 5. Verify and transfer the signed IPA

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="Android">

An Android APK/AAB cannot be uploaded as an iOS build. Follow the Google Play page for that platform. Apple listing preparation applies only when the product also has a separately built iOS application.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS">

Use the approved device archive/export and signing process. Preserve the exact bundle ID, version/build, signing evidence, symbols, source commit, and approval references.

If you have the actual archived `.app` locally, inspect its identifiers on macOS:

```bash
APP_BUNDLE='REPLACE_WITH_ACTUAL_ARCHIVED_APP_BUNDLE_PATH'
/usr/libexec/PlistBuddy -c 'Print :CFBundleIdentifier' "$APP_BUNDLE/Info.plist"
/usr/libexec/PlistBuddy -c 'Print :CFBundleShortVersionString' "$APP_BUNDLE/Info.plist"
/usr/libexec/PlistBuddy -c 'Print :CFBundleVersion' "$APP_BUNDLE/Info.plist"
```

Match these values to the exported IPA's release evidence. Inspecting one archive does not prove a different IPA came from it. Use [CI/CD App Signing](../cicd/signing-and-credentials.mdx) for signing verification.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="React Native">

Use the **iOS native archive/export** from the approved workflow. A Metro bundle, Android build, or successful TypeScript check does not establish iOS signing or upload readiness.

Apply the iOS identity verification above. Retain matching JavaScript source maps and native dSYMs for the shipped iOS version. Use [Datadog symbol guidance](../observability/datadog-mobile-integration.mdx) for the protected upload integration.

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

Transfer the identified signed IPA to the release team using the approved Syncplicity route or other authorized Enterprise file-transfer service. Include the completed listing/assets, release/approval record, and secure reviewer-access reference. Do not create an unrestricted public download link.

## 6. Upload using Transporter or the approved automation

The authorized owner selects **one approved upload route** for this candidate. Do not manually upload a duplicate merely because an automated upload has an uncertain result.

### Transporter on macOS

1. Install/open Apple's **Transporter** application from the Mac App Store through the approved workstation process.
2. Sign in with the authorized account and confirm the intended provider/team where applicable.
3. Add the identified signed IPA using Transporter's file-add flow.
4. Review validation messages. Resolve the actual signing, package, or metadata error rather than suppressing it.
5. Use the delivery action to upload. Record the delivery status and inspect errors/warnings.
6. Open the intended app in App Store Connect and wait for processing to complete. Match the processed build to the bundle ID, version, and build number.

Transporter supports IPA delivery; Apple also supports Xcode and other documented upload methods. [Apple upload methods and processing](https://developer.apple.com/help/app-store-connect/manage-builds/upload-builds), [Transporter submission guide](https://support.apple.com/en-in/guide/transporter-app/apdac1c9a477/mac).

### Existing Bitrise upload

Use the approved App Store Connect credential binding and deployment workflow from [CI/CD Delivery & Verification](../cicd/delivery-and-verification.mdx). That page owns the deployment YAML; do not introduce another credential-upload procedure here.

An upload configured to skip metadata/screenshots still needs complete store metadata/assets through the approved submission process. Disabling automatic review submission does not automatically assign testers or publish the app.

**Attach screenshot — sanitized delivery result and matching processed build in App Store Connect.**

## 7. Submit for review and verify release

