import { Tabs, TabItem } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import MermaidZoom from '../components/mermaid-zoom.astro';

Use this page for Android distribution through **Google Play**. Internal testing on Play is separate from the [Enterprise App Store](./enterprise-app-store.mdx).

**Before starting:** confirm the application ID, destination track, authorized release owner, and required approvals in [Signing & Release Approval](./signing-and-release-approval.mdx).

**Outcome:** the approved Android version reaches its intended Play testers or production audience, with signing, review, rollout, and visibility verified separately.

> **Confidential — Enterprise internal use only.** Keep release keys, service-account JSON, reviewer passwords, tester lists, binaries, and private access links in approved systems. Redact screenshots. Enter required review credentials only through the approved secure handoff and restricted Play review fields.

## 1. Identify the app and intended track

1. Open the approved **Play Console** account and locate the existing app. For a new application, the authorized owner creates its record and completes the required setup tasks.
2. Confirm the exact Android application ID, version name, version code, source commit, and release owner.
3. Select the intended destination with that owner:

| Track | Intended use |
| :--- | :--- |
| Internal testing | Restricted initial testing with an approved tester audience. |
| Closed testing | Testing by configured groups on the selected closed track. |
| Open testing | Broader opt-in testing; requires readiness for that visibility. |
| Production | Approved public release in the selected countries/regions. |

Do not create a duplicate app record for an update. Keep the approved `com.enterprise.<app-name>` identity and actual environment mapping. A visible internal-test release does not establish a production release. [App setup](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/9859152?hl=en), [testing tracks](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/9845334?hl=en).

**Attach screenshot — intended app and selected track, with private account/tester details hidden.**

## 2. Prepare the listing information

Open the app's **Store listings** area and complete the approved default/localized listing content. Follow the actual console navigation if its labels differ.

| Field | Required preparation |
| :--- | :--- |
| App name | Approved public name, **up to 30 characters**. |
| Short description | Summary, **up to 80 characters**. |
| Full description | Feature/functionality description, **up to 4,000 characters**. |
| Languages | Approved primary/default listing language and localized content. |
| Category | The appropriate category available in Play Console, approved by the application/content owner. |
| Privacy policy | Live public policy URL applicable to the app. |
| Support/contact details | Actual approved user-support information required by the listing. |
| Countries/regions | Approved availability for the selected distribution route. |

Use relevant search terms naturally in accurate listing text. Do not invent a separate Apple-style keyword field for Play, or use repetitive keyword stuffing. [Play listing fields and limits](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/9859152?hl=en).

Copyable preparation record—not an API/upload schema:

```text
Application ID: [Exact registered package]
Public app name: [Approved localized text; maximum 30 characters]
Short description: [Approved localized text; maximum 80 characters]
Full description: [Approved localized text; maximum 4,000 characters]
Listing languages: [Approved locales]
App category: [Actual Play Console selection]
Privacy policy URL: [Approved live public policy]
Support/contact information: [Approved public details]
Track and countries/audience: [Explicit approved scope]
Content owner: [Responsible owner]
```

## 3. Prepare graphics and screenshots

| Asset | Specification |
| :--- | :--- |
| High-resolution store icon | **512 × 512 px**, 32-bit PNG with alpha; maximum **1,024 KB**. |
| Feature graphic | **1,024 × 500 px**, JPEG or 24-bit PNG without alpha. |
| Standard phone/tablet screenshots | JPEG or 24-bit PNG without alpha; minimum dimension **320 px**, maximum **3,840 px**; larger dimension no more than twice the smaller. |

Prepare at least **two screenshots** under Play's applicable listing requirements and include the required assets for each supported form factor. Additional form-factor requirements and recommendations can differ; use the current specification for the actual device categories. Supply intended order and approved localized assets. [Google Play preview-asset specifications](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/9866151?hl=en).

1. Capture representative screens of the actual candidate using synthetic data.
2. Check dimensions, format, transparency, and supported form factor.
3. Review the images for customer data, account identifiers, private endpoints, and misleading functionality claims.
4. Upload to the corresponding listing asset sections and inspect the resulting preview.

On macOS, inspect a local asset before handoff:

```bash
ASSET_PATH='REPLACE_WITH_ACTUAL_ASSET_PATH'
sips -g pixelWidth -g pixelHeight -g format -g hasAlpha "$ASSET_PATH"
stat -f '%z bytes' "$ASSET_PATH"
```

Compare the output with that asset's requirements. These commands do not validate image bit depth, visual quality, policy compliance, or console acceptance.

Suggested secure handoff folders:

```text
<approved-release-handoff>/
├── listing/<locale>/       # Approved app name and descriptions
├── graphics/              # Store icon and feature graphic
├── screenshots/<locale>/  # Supported form factors and intended order
└── release-record.txt     # Identity, approvals, secure artifact reference
```

**Attach screenshot — sanitized store listing preview and ordered graphic assets.**

## 4. Complete reviewer access and app-content requirements

### Reviewer sign-in

1. Verify a dedicated test account works with the actual candidate and review environment. Keep it active and unlocked, with sufficient access to review the submitted features.
2. Open the app's **App content** area and its sign-in/access instructions section. Use the current console label.
3. The authorized owner enters the required access details and explains any extra login steps, restricted screens, or special setup.
4. Save the instructions. For updated review information, confirm the changes are included in the actual review submission; saving alone may not send them for review.

Do not use a real employee/customer account or place passwords in a public listing. [Prepare the app for review and provide access](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/9859455?hl=en).

### Privacy and policy declarations

Complete the console's applicable content-rating, audience, ads, permissions, privacy, and other required declarations with the responsible owners. Base answers on the shipped app, not a generic template.

For public production and applicable testing routes, complete **Data safety** using actual app and third-party SDK behavior. Apps exclusively active on the internal-testing track have a documented Data safety exemption; that does not remove Enterprise privacy or security obligations. [Data safety requirements and scope](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/10787469?hl=en).

Check required setup tasks and any account-specific production testing requirements in the app dashboard. Do not assume eligibility to publish production because a bundle was accepted on an internal track. [Play testing setup](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/9845334?hl=en).

**Attach screenshot — sanitized App content/access status, with reviewer credentials hidden.**

## 5. Confirm the artifact and signing handoff

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="Android">

For new Play releases, use the approved **Android App Bundle (AAB)** route. APK support for applicable existing apps depends on the app's established Play configuration; do not select APK merely because an internal-store build produced one. [Play release preparation](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/9859348?hl=en).

If the release manager requests an unsigned artifact, provide it through the approved secure route for signing. Before Play upload, the candidate must use the registered upload-signing identity and match the intended package/version code. The release owner maintains the actual key configuration. [Play App Signing](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/9842756).

Follow [CI/CD App Signing](../cicd/signing-and-credentials.mdx) for protected credential bindings and signed-output verification. Do not generate a replacement production key, re-sign a candidate, or upload raw keystore credentials as a developer workaround.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS">

An iOS IPA cannot be uploaded to Google Play. Follow [Apple App Store](./apple-app-store.mdx) for the iOS application. Shared product branding does not make the platform release identities interchangeable.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="React Native">

Use the Android native AAB built by the approved React Native workflow. A JavaScript bundle, Metro session, or successful iOS archive is not a Play artifact.

Confirm the final Android application ID, version name/code, source revision, upload-signing result, and native build configuration. Retain matching JS source maps and applicable Android mappings for [crash investigation](../observability/monitoring-and-troubleshooting.mdx).

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

Provide the secure candidate reference, exact identity, signing evidence, and required approval records to the release owner. The spreadsheet/request process remains in [Signing & Release Approval](./signing-and-release-approval.mdx).

## 6. Prepare the first upload and subsequent automation

### Initial Play preparation

1. The authorized owner confirms the app record, Play App Signing setup, initial dashboard requirements, and intended track.
2. For the established Bitrise deployment path, confirm the required initial console upload has been completed before subsequent automated deployments.
3. In the selected track, create the release and add the identified signed candidate through the approved upload route.
4. Inspect the parsed package/version information and validation results. Correct actual signing, version-code, SDK, policy, or device-compatibility errors before continuing.
5. Add accurate release notes and complete the required review/rollout steps for that track.

The initial-console-upload prerequisite belongs to the approved deployment integration. Do not generalize it into a claim that every Google API workflow requires the same bootstrap steps. [Play release flow](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/9859348?hl=en).

### Existing Bitrise deployment

Use [CI/CD Delivery & Verification](../cicd/delivery-and-verification.mdx) for the reviewed deployment YAML, protected service-account binding, signed AAB input, and destination checks. This page does not duplicate that credential provisioning or pipeline configuration.

A successful `google-play-deploy` step does not by itself establish that reviewers approved the app or that testers can install the candidate. If an upload outcome is uncertain, preserve the build evidence and reconcile the destination before repeating a write.

**Attach screenshot — sanitized uploaded candidate, version code, and track/release state.**

