title: "Lost-Device Data Wipe"
description: "Authorized administrator procedure for selecting a lost/stolen device, requesting application data removal, and recording the verified outcome in Digital.ai App Management."
sidebar:
  label: Lost-Device Data Wipe
  order: 6
---

import { Tabs, TabItem } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import MermaidZoom from '../components/mermaid-zoom.astro';

Use this procedure when a reported lost, stolen, or decommissioned device requires removal of an Enterprise application's local data through **Digital.ai App Management (Apperian)**.

**Operator:** an authorized administrator with access to the correct environment and user/device/application records. Ordinary developers and end users report the issue; they do not execute this operation as an installation fix.

**Outcome:** request removal for the exact target application/device and record whether completion is confirmed, pending, or unknown.

> **Confidential — Enterprise internal use only.** Protect incident details, user/device identifiers, console links, and administrative evidence. This operation deletes application data. Use the actual incident authorization and target scope; do not run it on a real employee device merely to test this documentation.

## 1. Confirm scope and prerequisites

| Confirm before acting | Why |
| :--- | :--- |
| Reported user and exact device | A user can have multiple registered devices. |
| Correct QA/UAT/production environment | Similar names can exist in different stores. |
| Exact target application(s) | The request applies to selected apps, not automatically every app on the device. |
| Administrator's authorization | Console access alone is not the incident's target/scope decision. |
| Data Wipe policy on the installed app | The policy must be effective for the targeted managed application. |

Have the application/platform owner confirm policy readiness. If applying/changing a policy requires re-signing and distributing a new version, an already installed older build must not be assumed protected. Do not attempt to retrofit a lost device by changing a release without owner review. [Policy prerequisites](https://docs.digital.ai/app-management/docs/delete-application-data-from-a-device).

If the user/device cannot be identified reliably, stop target selection and resolve the ambiguity through the responsible incident/device owner.

## 2. Understand what the operation removes

The Data Wipe policy removes the selected managed application's supported local data and quits the application when the wipe executes. It is **not a whole-device factory reset** and does not delete backend/server records.

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="Android">

The vendor documents removal of the application's directory contents, associated shared preferences, databases, and cache. Quitting clears the application's active in-memory process state.

External storage, such as an SD card, is outside the documented wipe scope. NDK linkage files are also excluded. Do not report those locations as cleared without a separately verified mechanism.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS">

The vendor documents removal of app-associated user defaults, Keychain items, HTTP cookies, Documents contents, and cache. Quitting clears active in-memory process state.

Do not extend this claim to every shared Keychain group, another app's storage, backups, or remote services. Verify the actual managed application's policy/integration rather than assuming all account data everywhere is gone.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="React Native">

The managed Android/iOS package and its effective policy determine the wipe behavior. React Native does not introduce a separate universal remote-wipe operation.

Identify the actual platform package and its registered device. Confirm the app's storage inventory with the application owner, including native-backed storage and any data outside the documented policy scope. Do not replace this administrative operation with a JavaScript Redux reset or an assumed storage API call.

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

[Digital.ai Data Wipe scope](https://docs.digital.ai/app-management/docs/data-wipe). Application-level account cleanup has its own contracts in [App Foundations](../enterprise-app-foundations.mdx); it is not evidence that this remote operation completed.

## 3. Request Clear Data in the administrator console

**Where:** the approved Apperian/Digital.ai administration console for the selected environment.

1. Sign in using the authorized administrator account and select **QA**, **UAT**, or **production** as required by the incident.
2. Open **Users** and locate the reported user. Open their details.
3. In the registered-device list, select the **exact reported device**. Compare its available device details with the incident record; do not select by a similar display name alone.
4. Open that device's application list and select only the authorized target application(s).
5. Inspect the selection again. Do not select the App Catalog client itself.
6. Click **Clear Data**. Read the confirmation and confirm only when the user, device, environment, and app selection match the authorized scope.
7. Inspect and record the resulting application **Data Status**, time, and selected target. Do not assume the request completed when the dialog closes.

If **Clear Data** is disabled, investigate policy eligibility and selection with the platform owner; do not broaden the selection or change access controls to enable the button. [Administrator console procedure](https://docs.digital.ai/app-management/docs/delete-application-data-from-a-device).

**Attach screenshot — sanitized environment, selected device/application, confirmation, and resulting Data Status. Never expose another user's device details.**

## 4. Distinguish request submission from execution

The policy is evaluated when the user:

1. Opens the application.
2. Brings it from the background to the foreground.
3. Returns to it from the device's lock screen.

The wipe occurs when the managed app processes the applicable policy, not necessarily at the instant the administrator clicks the button. These triggers do not establish a guaranteed completion time for a powered-off, offline, unreachable, or never-reopened device. [Policy evaluation and user experience](https://docs.digital.ai/app-management/docs/data-wipe).

When the documented wipe executes, the application displays:

```text
The data for this application has been wiped,quitting...
```

The application quits. A later launch can open it again without the previously retained data covered by the wipe policy. A wipe is not a permanent app-access revocation; any required account/device/session containment remains a separate owner-managed process.

| Console observation | Report |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Marked for Clear Data** | Request recorded; completion remains pending. |
| **Clear Data Successful** | Console reports completion for the selected app/device; retain its evidence and time. |
| Timeout, unexpected response, or no reliable status | Outcome unknown; inspect the existing record and escalate. |

These status names are documented by Digital.ai. Use the actual installed console's state and supporting record; do not invent an acknowledgement or map an unfamiliar state to success. [Data Status transitions](https://docs.digital.ai/app-management/docs/delete-application-data-from-a-device).

## 5. Verify and record the outcome

<MermaidZoom title="A requested wipe is pending until its outcome is evidenced">

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["Authorized incident and exact target"] --> B["Confirm environment, device, app and effective policy"]
    B --> C["Submit Clear Data and confirm selection"]
    C --> D["Read existing Data Status"]
    D --> E{"Observed outcome"}
    E -->|Marked for Clear Data| F["Record pending; monitor policy execution"]
    E -->|Clear Data Successful| G["Record console completion evidence"]
    E -->|Unknown| H["Preserve records; reconcile read-only with owner"]
    F --> D
    G --> I["Report verified scope and remaining limits"]
    classDef prepare fill:#172554,stroke:#93c5fd,color:#fff;
    classDef operation fill:#4c1d95,stroke:#d8b4fe,color:#fff;
    classDef state fill:#713f12,stroke:#fde047,color:#fff;
    classDef evidence fill:#064e3b,stroke:#6ee7b7,color:#fff;
    class A,B prepare;
    class C,D operation;
    class E,F,H state;
    class G,I evidence;
```

</MermaidZoom>

1. Reopen/read the selected device/application record and inspect the current status.
2. Record the actual status and observation time. If completion is not evidenced, retain **pending** or **unknown** rather than “wiped.”
3. For a controlled test on an authorized test device, verify the documented local-data outcome with the application owner. A signed-out screen or a successful relaunch alone does not prove every storage category was cleared.
4. Report the confirmed scope and exclusions through the approved incident/support route. A stolen device may not be available for local inspection; distinguish console-reported completion from a physical-device verification.

Copyable evidence record—not an API payload:

```text
Incident/reference: [Approved record]
Authorized operator: [Responsible administrator]
Environment: [QA / UAT / production]
Target user/device: [Approved internal identifiers; restrict access]
Target application/package: [Exact managed application]
Policy readiness: [Owner-confirmed state or unresolved]
Request time: [Time and time zone]
Observed Data Status: [Exact console value]
Observation time: [Time and time zone]
Completion evidence: [Approved console/audit reference or unavailable]
Device inspection: [Performed on authorized test device / unavailable]
Remaining scope/limitations: [External storage, server data, unknowns]
Responsible follow-up owner: [Assigned owner]
```

No API, shell deletion command, or application-side force-quit code is supplied here: the documented operation is the authorized console policy workflow. Do not implement a new wipe endpoint or use local test cleanup as a substitute.

## 6. Handle blocked or uncertain results

| Observation | Action |
| :--- | :--- |
| User/device match is uncertain | Stop and resolve the exact target before selecting an app. |
| Clear Data disabled | Have the owner confirm the effective policy and selected app eligibility. |
| Request remains marked/pending | Record pending and investigate device/policy evaluation with the platform/device owner. Do not promise instant deletion. |
| Network timeout after confirmation | Inspect the existing target's status read-only before any further mutation; do not repeatedly click Clear Data. |
| Console reports success but an authorized test still finds scoped data | Preserve the evidence and escalate the discrepancy. Do not broaden deletion to other apps or storage as an assumed fix. |
| Server records or external storage remain | They are outside this operation's stated scope; route the requirement to the responsible owner. |

Do not delete the application/user/device record, uninstall the app, revoke signing material, or rerun provisioning to force a desired status. Follow the assigned incident process and [Safe Recovery](../cicd/troubleshooting-and-recovery.mdx) for uncertain external outcomes.

